1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:06,315
As autonomous A-I agents take over on-chain trading and data pipelines, they run into two missing primitives.

2
00:00:06,315 --> 00:00:13,256
First: how do they pay for specialized micro-services without centralized credit cards or custodial API accounts?

3
00:00:13,256 --> 00:00:20,339
And second: how do downstream contracts verify that an agent's audit was actually computed against canonical on-chain state?

4
00:00:20,339 --> 00:00:22,719
This is Sentinel four-oh-two Gateway.

5
00:00:22,719 --> 00:00:31,853
An autonomous liquidity auditor powered by the x-four-oh-two Micropayment Protocol, The Graph Subgraph Studio, and E-N-S v-two permissioned attestations.

6
00:00:31,853 --> 00:00:35,855
Let's see what happens when an unauthorized agent requests an on-demand audit.

7
00:00:35,855 --> 00:00:45,449
When we trigger the endpoint without payment, the gateway immediately intercepts the call and returns an R-F-C compliant H-T-T-P four-oh-two Payment Required challenge.

8
00:00:45,449 --> 00:00:54,506
Notice the response: it specifies the exact micropayment required — point zero zero one E-T-H — along with the Bazantic facilitator address.

9
00:00:54,506 --> 00:00:57,138
No credit cards, no pre-shared secrets.

10
00:00:57,138 --> 00:01:01,644
Any autonomous agent can parse this header and settle payment programmatically.

11
00:01:01,644 --> 00:01:06,644
Now, we pass a valid Bazantic M-P-P session token and re-execute.

12
00:01:06,644 --> 00:01:12,872
The gateway verifies the credential against the facilitator and dispatches Step Two of our declarative Bazantic recipe.

13
00:01:12,872 --> 00:01:19,714
Here, Sentinel four-oh-two queries The Graph Subgraph Studio for live mainnet Uniswap v-three pool data.

14
00:01:19,714 --> 00:01:27,807
In real time, it indexes total value locked, fee tiers, and volume velocity, calculating a comprehensive liquidity risk score.

15
00:01:27,807 --> 00:01:40,230
Most importantly, the agent serializes this state into a canonical payload and computes a deterministic Keccak two-fifty-six report hash, creating a tamper-proof cryptographic fingerprint of the audited liquidity.

16
00:01:40,230 --> 00:01:43,695
In Step Three, we lock this audit permanently on-chain.

17
00:01:43,695 --> 00:01:57,182
Using E-N-S v-two on Ethereum Sepolia, the agent connects to the official Permissioned Resolver and calls set text, binding the audit hash directly to the subname auditor dot sentinel four-oh-two dot eth, under the key records last audit hash.

18
00:01:57,182 --> 00:02:06,962
Any De-Fi protocol or smart contract can now inspect this E-N-S record to verify that liquidity depth was independently validated before routing high-value transactions.

19
00:02:06,962 --> 00:02:13,607
For A-I agent developers, Sentinel four-oh-two exposes this entire lifecycle over the Model Context Protocol.

20
00:02:13,607 --> 00:02:25,131
Tools like Claude Desktop, Cursor, or autonomous agent frameworks can call audit pool liquidity or get E-N-S attestation natively via standard I-O or H-T-T-P JSON-R-P-C.

21
00:02:25,131 --> 00:02:35,449
We've also built an interactive Demo Studio right into the frontend, letting judges replay every stage, inspect raw payloads, and export signed audit runs.

22
00:02:35,449 --> 00:02:47,982
Sentinel four-oh-two Gateway brings together machine-payable A-P-Is, real-time decentralized indexing, and verifiable on-chain identity, building the trust layer for the next generation of autonomous on-chain agents.

23
00:02:47,982 --> 00:02:54,232
Thank you, and check out the open-source code on GitHub at n-l-n-w slash eth-online-twenty-twenty-six.

